{
  "case_version": 1,
  "name": "loop-ttl-and-branch-receipts",
  "description": "The topology contains a real cycle: px-a forwards L0 onto a-b, px-b rewrites L1 to L3 and sends the marker back over b-a, and px-a accepts L3 and forwards it onto a-b again. A marker that enters the cycle loses TTL on every hop and is finally discarded by expiry, never delivered from the loop. The request is a fan-out with a retry: each injection produces one independent thread per egress link of px-a, and a retry produces a second copy of each thread. The direct branch delivers twice, so the second delivery is recorded as a duplicate for that branch with the kept receipt named and a reason; the looping branch only ever expires, so it never touches the direct branch's receipt. This is the guarantee that branches of one request cannot overwrite or mix receipts.",
